---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { getBuildMode } from '../../../lib/build-mode.js';

const mode = getBuildMode();
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Low Carbon Energy & Biofuels | Terra Nexus"
  description="Terra Nexus helps biofuel producers and commodity traders create value at every step — from field-level carbon-intensity analysis and origination through commercialization, program management, and verification."
  preview={mode === 'preview'}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">SOIL TO CASH</p>
    <h1>Create More Value from Low-Carbon Feedstocks</h1>
    <p class="hero-lead">Terra Nexus helps biofuel producers and commodity traders create value at every step — from field-level carbon-intensity analysis and origination through commercialization, program management, and verification.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/">Discuss Your Next Step</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/capabilities/">Explore Our Capabilities</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Definition and Scope</h2>
      <p>Low-Carbon Energy &amp; Biofuels focuses on the energy and biofuels value chain — connecting field-level evidence, feedstock carbon-intensity calculations, origination and aggregation, chain-of-custody and attribute allocation, fuel pathway and facility economics, contracts and value sharing, recurring operating controls, and audit, tax, and verification requirements.</p>
      <h3>Topic Boundaries</h3>
      <ul class="topic-boundaries">
        <li>Other Areas of Expertise address specific production systems</li>
        <li>This topic focuses on the energy and biofuels value chain</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Why This Topic Matters</h2>
      <blockquote class="market-thesis">Low-carbon fuel value is not created by a carbon-intensity calculation alone. Durable value depends on connecting field-level evidence, feedstock CI calculations, origination/aggregation/movement, chain-of-custody and attribute allocation, fuel pathway/facility economics, contracts and value sharing, recurring operating controls, and audit/tax/verification requirements. A favorable CI score becomes commercially useful only when all elements operate together and withstand independent review.</blockquote>
      <p>A favorable carbon-intensity score is necessary but not sufficient. The score must be supported by field-level evidence that can withstand audit, connected to a fuel pathway and facility economics that justify the investment, translated into contracts that allocate value fairly among producers, aggregators, and refiners, and operated through controls that maintain eligibility across reporting periods. When any link is missing, the value slips away — through disallowed feedstocks, failed verification, broken mass balance, or contractual gaps that leave parties without recourse.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Primary Value-Chain Position</h2>
      <p><strong>Energy &amp; Biofuels Refiners; Commodity Trading; Agricultural Production.</strong> U.S.-led; an agricultural feedstock and carbon-intensity operating challenge across eligible fuel pathways.</p>
      <p>The value chain runs from the field where feedstock carbon intensity is determined, through origination and aggregation, into refining and fuel production, and out to the credit, tax, and compliance markets where low-carbon value is monetized. Each node must be designed to preserve evidence and eligibility.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Primary Audiences</h2>
      <div class="audience-grid">
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Biofuel Producers</h3>
          <p>Co-lead audience. Producers must connect feedstock CI economics, fuel pathway performance, facility operations, regulatory credit value, and audit and verification readiness into a coherent commercial and operating model.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Commodity Traders &amp; Originators</h3>
          <p>Co-lead audience. Originators connect producer relationships, feedstock evidence, aggregation, mass balance, movement, and contractual representations to refiner demand and credit markets.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Agricultural Producers</h3>
          <p>Eligible practices, field-level evidence, enrollment economics, and alignment with downstream feedstock and credit requirements.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Enabling Tech &amp; Solution Providers</h3>
          <p>Market entry, product strategy, technical diligence, pilot design, partner selection, and integration into origination and operating workflows.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Private Equity, Venture Capital &amp; Impact Investors</h3>
          <p>Technology and transaction diligence, business-case modeling, portfolio and capital decisions across the low-carbon fuel landscape.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="audience-card">
          <h3>Environmental Markets &amp; Ecosystem Services</h3>
          <p>Market-mechanism and methodology assessment, credit and attribute design, verification, and claims translation across overlapping programs.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Decisions Clients Face</h2>
      <div class="decisions-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Strategy &amp; Commercial Owners</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Feedstock and facility carbon-intensity economics</li>
            <li>Sourcing regions, crops, practices, and supply scenarios</li>
            <li>Reduced-CI feedstock product and market strategy</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Origination &amp; Supply</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Producer enrollment and field-level evidence requirements</li>
            <li>Aggregation, mass balance, and eligible-volume controls</li>
            <li>Premiums, value sharing, and contractual representations</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Pathway &amp; Regulatory</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Carbon-intensity pathway analysis and scenario comparison</li>
            <li>Tax, transaction, and verification readiness</li>
            <li>Auditor and verifier documentation</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Program, Operations &amp; Technology Teams</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>How do field evidence, CI calculations, ledgers, and controls connect?</li>
            <li>What data to collect directly versus model?</li>
            <li>What to internalize, outsource, or run as a managed service?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Carbon Intensity Analysis</h2>
      <p>Terra Nexus provides field-level and management-unit-level carbon-intensity analysis, scenario comparison, and value-allocation modeling to support feedstock sourcing, program design, and audit readiness. This analysis is a decision-support tool. It does not replace official USDA FD-CIC tools, DOE 45ZCF-GREET models, tax advice, certification, or independent verification.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Representative Value Pathways</h2>
      <p>The same underlying feedstock and practice improvement may support different claims with different levels of rigor, cost, commercial reach, and value. The best pathway is not always the claim with the highest nominal price.</p>
      <div class="pathway-tags">
        <span class="pathway-tag">Low-CI feedstock premiums</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Regulatory credit value (RFS/RINs, LCFS, 45Z)</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Product differentiation and customer qualification</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Scope 3 and chain-of-custody claims</span>
        <span class="pathway-tag">Supply-resilience and origination value</span>
      </div>
      <p class="pathway-note">These are categories of potential value, not promises that every pathway is available, stackable, transferable, or commercially superior. Detailed pathway analysis remains client-specific.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How Terra Nexus Capabilities Apply</h2>
      <div class="capability-mapping">
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/strategy-and-innovation/">Strategy &amp; Innovation</a></h3>
          <p>Market, growth, business-model, product, program, and partner strategy; opportunity assessment, segmentation, positioning, and commercialization; pilot design and build-buy-partner decisions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/financial-investments-and-new-venture-development/">Financial Investments &amp; New Venture Development</a></h3>
          <p>Business cases, scenario analysis, value allocation, and initiative prioritization; investor, technology, project, program, and transaction diligence; capital, partnership, and portfolio decisions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/sustainable-supply-chain-and-operations/">Sustainable Supply Chain &amp; Operations</a></h3>
          <p>Sourcing, supplier, producer, traceability, and chain-of-custody design; operating models, governance, systems, controls, and implementation; recurring program management.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/corporate-sustainability/">Corporate Sustainability</a></h3>
          <p>Impact measurement, inventory and target alignment, disclosure, and governance; product, value-chain, climate, nature, resilience, and claims integration; stakeholder communication.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3><a href="/capabilities/carbon-and-ecosystem-services/">Carbon &amp; Ecosystem Services</a></h3>
          <p>Market-mechanism and methodology assessment; environmental-program design, MRV, valuation, commercialization, and management; verification readiness, evidence, and claims translation.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How Terra Nexus Engages</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Advise</h3>
          <p>Terra Nexus evaluates opportunities, performs diligence, models economics, defines strategy, selects frameworks and partners, and designs programs, systems, controls, and commercial structures.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Manage</h3>
          <p>Terra Nexus embeds within client-owned programs to coordinate recurring evidence, data, calculations, ledgers, partners, exceptions, reporting, and verification readiness.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-dark">
    <div class="container text-center">
      <h2>Start Your Soil-to-Cash Journey</h2>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/">Discuss Your Next Step</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .topic-boundaries { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .topic-boundaries li { padding: var(--space-2) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .topic-boundaries li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .market-thesis { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-lg); font-style: italic; color: var(--color-text); padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .audience-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .audience-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .audience-card { padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--color-bg); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); }
  .audience-card h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .audience-card p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .decisions-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-6); grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .decisions-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .decisions-grid h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .decisions-grid ul { padding-left: var(--space-5); }
  .decisions-grid li { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .pathway-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  .pathway-tag { font-size: var(--text-xs); padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3); background: var(--color-bg); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .pathway-note { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); font-style: italic; }
  .capability-mapping { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) { .capability-mapping { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .capability-mapping article { padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); }
  .capability-mapping h3 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .capability-mapping h3 a { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .capability-mapping p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .text-center { text-align: center; }
  .text-center .hero-actions { justify-content: center; }
  .section-dark .btn-primary { background: #fff; color: #1a1f24; border-color: #fff; }
  .section-dark .btn-primary:hover { background: #e2e5e9; border-color: #e2e5e9; }
</style>
